# Local DNS and DHCP configuration template - MULE
#
# Serves DNS and DHCP to end user devices associated with the EUD access point,
# so that an operator's phone can find the node's services by name without
# configuration.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Every value below is TBD. This file is an input to
# configuration generation, not a file to be edited on a node. dnsmasq is the
# assumed implementation; that choice is not recorded as an ADR and should be.
#
# Trades: TBR-NET-05 (the EUD AP subnet, the DHCP range and the lease).
# NOT TBR-NET-01, which is CLOSED and decided the per-deployment MESH prefix
# only; docs/evidence/TBR-NET-01/README.md says so directly.
#
# KNOWN PROBLEM with the scoping below, recorded 2026-10-03: SAD section 4.3
# bridges EUD access into the BATMAN domain and FML-ADR-056 puts the AP
# interfaces in the mesh bridge. dnsmasq then binds the BRIDGE, and the mesh is
# inside it -- so `interface=<ap>` plus `bind-interfaces` has no boundary left
# to scope on and cannot honour the "do NOT serve onto the mesh" rule below by
# itself. See docs/evidence/TBR-NET-05/2026-10-03-ap-subnet-and-dhcp-decision.md.
#         TBR-TAK-01 (which services need names)
#
# LOCAL-FIRST
#
# The system must remain useful with no internet, no central server, and no
# parent infrastructure. This resolver is authoritative for the local domain
# and forwards nothing by default. A resolver that fails when an upstream is
# unreachable would defeat the property the whole program exists for.

# --- Scope ------------------------------------------------------------------
interface=TBD                  # EUD access point interface only.
bind-interfaces
# Do NOT serve DNS or DHCP onto the mesh. Every node runs this service, and
# several DHCP servers on one flat layer 2 broadcast domain is a fault that
# presents as intermittent and is unpleasant to diagnose. batman-adv makes the
# mesh one broadcast domain; see batman-adv.conf.template.

# --- DHCP -------------------------------------------------------------------
# FROM THE MISSION PACKAGE AND THE ADDRESSING SCHEME. Never literals here.
# dhcp-range=TBD
# dhcp-option=TBD              # Router and DNS options
# Lease time TBD: short enough that a device moving between nodes recovers
# quickly, long enough not to churn. Nobody has measured what that is.

# --- DNS --------------------------------------------------------------------
# domain=TBD                   # Local domain. From the mission package.
# local=/TBD/                  # Authoritative for the local domain.

no-resolv
# Forward nothing by default. Local-first; see above.

# Service names resolve to this node's addresses. Which services exist and
# which need names is TBR-TAK-01 and services/catalog/. See services/ingress/.
# address=/TBD/TBD

# --- What must never appear here --------------------------------------------
# No real domain, hostname, member identity, callsign, or deployment location,
# in any form, including in a comment or an example. A DNS configuration
# discloses the structure of a deployment and the names of its participants.
# See SECURITY.md, publication rule.

# --- Notes ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two independently built deployments meeting at an incident must not collide.
# A fixed local domain across all deployments makes that collision certain.
#
# NO TRADE OWNS THE LOCAL DOMAIN, checked 2026-10-03. TBR-NET-01 is CLOSED and
# decided the mesh prefix; TBR-NET-05 owns the AP subnet and DHCP; TBR-NET-06
# owns how a REMOTE EUD resolves one service name across the WAN overlay. The
# local domain a phone on the AP subnet resolves is needed by this file, by the
# HAProxy hdr(host) ACL and by any ingress certificate SAN, and is owned by
# none of them. Recorded in services/ingress/README.md.
